.\" ip.7 - Substrate IPv4
.TH IP 7 "2026-09-26" "Substrate" "Substrate Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
ip \- IPv4 protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/socket.h>
.B #include <netinet/in.h>
.PP
.IB udp_socket " = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);"
.IB tcp_socket " = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);"
.IB raw_socket " = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_RAW, " protocol ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Substrate's IPv4 follows RFC 791 as amended by RFC 1122.
Each interface has one address and netmask; datagrams to a destination on
no local subnet go to the interface's gateway.
All of 127.0.0.0/8 is the host's own loopback, and a datagram to one of
the host's interface addresses never leaves the host.
.PP
The 0.0.0.0/8 block is never a destination: sending to an address in it
fails with
.BR EINVAL ,
except that 0.0.0.0 itself, as the destination of
.BR connect (2)
or
.BR sendto (2),
names the local host.
.PP
Received fragments are reassembled, up to eight datagrams at a time, each
for at most 30 seconds.
Datagrams are never fragmented on output: one larger than the path allows
fails with
.BR EMSGSIZE .
.PP
Options in received datagrams are checked; a malformed one draws an ICMP
Parameter Problem, and a datagram whose source route still has hops to
visit is discarded, as this host does not forward.
Record Route and Timestamp options in an ICMP echo request are updated
with this host and returned in the reply.
.SS Path MTU discovery
TCP discovers the path MTU (RFC 1191): its segments carry Don't Fragment,
and an ICMP "fragmentation needed" lowers the MTU recorded for the
destination, after which the connection sends smaller segments.
A recorded MTU expires after ten minutes.
Reports below 552 octets are held at 552 and the path is marked so that
.B IP_PMTUDISC_WANT
sockets send to it without Don't Fragment.
Other sockets set Don't Fragment only when asked with
.BR IP_MTU_DISCOVER .
.SS ICMP errors
An ICMP Destination Unreachable, Time Exceeded or Parameter Problem about
a datagram a socket sent is reported to that socket.
A UDP socket sees it as the error of its next call; a TCP connection still
being established is refused at once by a protocol or port unreachable,
and any other error is reported if the connection later gives up.
.SS Socket options
Options at level
.B IPPROTO_IP
are set with
.BR setsockopt (2)
and read with
.BR getsockopt (2).
.TP
.B IP_TOS
An
.IR int :
the Type of Service octet of every datagram sent.
.TP
.B IP_TTL
An
.IR int ,
1 to 255, or \-1 for the default of 64: the Time to Live of every unicast
or broadcast datagram sent.
.TP
.B IP_OPTIONS
Up to 40 octets of IP options sent in every datagram, padded with End of
Option List to a multiple of four; an empty list removes them.
A list that is not well formed fails with
.BR EINVAL .
On a TCP socket the segment size shrinks to make room.
.BR getsockopt ()
returns the list as set.
.TP
.B IP_MTU_DISCOVER
An
.IR int :
.B IP_PMTUDISC_DONT
sends without Don't Fragment;
.B IP_PMTUDISC_WANT
and
.B IP_PMTUDISC_DO
set it and refuse a datagram larger than the recorded path MTU with
.BR EMSGSIZE ;
.B IP_PMTUDISC_PROBE
sets it and sends regardless.
TCP sockets start with
.BR IP_PMTUDISC_WANT ,
all others with
.BR IP_PMTUDISC_DONT .
.TP
.B IP_PKTINFO
An
.IR int :
when nonzero,
.BR recvmsg (2)
on a datagram socket returns an
.B IP_PKTINFO
control message naming the interface and destination address of each
datagram.
.TP
.BR IP_MULTICAST_IF ", " IP_MULTICAST_TTL ", " IP_MULTICAST_LOOP
The interface (by address, or a
.I struct ip_mreqn
naming an interface index), the Time to Live (default 1) and the local
loopback (default on) of datagrams sent to a multicast group.
.TP
.BR IP_ADD_MEMBERSHIP ", " IP_DROP_MEMBERSHIP
A
.I struct ip_mreq
or
.IR "struct ip_mreqn" :
join or leave a multicast group on an interface.
.PP
Any other
.B IPPROTO_IP
option, among them
.BR IP_HDRINCL ,
.BR IP_RECVERR
and the source-specific multicast options, fails with
.BR ENOPROTOOPT .
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EADDRNOTAVAIL
The source address is not one of this host's addresses, or is 0.0.0.0
for a multicast destination.
.TP
.B EINVAL
The destination is in 0.0.0.0/8; a 127.0.0.0/8 source was given for a
destination off the loopback interface; or an option value is invalid.
.TP
.B EMSGSIZE
The datagram is larger than the interface MTU, or, with Don't Fragment
set, than the recorded path MTU.
.TP
.B ENETUNREACH
There is no route to the destination.
.TP
.B ENOBUFS
The loopback interface's queue was full and the datagram was dropped.
.TP
.B ENOPROTOOPT
The
.B IPPROTO_IP
option is not implemented.
.SH EXAMPLE
Send a UDP datagram with Don't Fragment set:
.PP
.EX
int s = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
int mode = IP_PMTUDISC_DO;
setsockopt(s, IPPROTO_IP, IP_MTU_DISCOVER, &mode, sizeof(mode));
sendto(s, buf, len, 0, (struct sockaddr *)&dst, sizeof(dst));
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR socket (2),
.BR setsockopt (2),
.BR recvmsg (2),
.BR tcp (7)
